            <ArticleTitle>Patients' pain experience following oral mucosal biopsy under local anaesthesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>33-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine the incidence and severity of post-operative pain and use of analgesics following biopsy of oral mucosal lesions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A patient survey using a self-completed pain diary.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Seventy-six patients attending an oral medicine clinic for investigation of oral mucosal disease recorded overall and worst pain experiences and analgesic usage over 7 post-operative days following biopsy using visual analogue scales in patient completed diaries.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">One third of patients reported no post-operative pain on any day. Most patients recorded no pain or mild pain. The percentage experiencing pain dropped from the first day (61%) to the seventh day (21%). Most patients did not use any analgesics. Analgesic use was not related to the presence of pain. Only a small minority of patients recorded severe pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Biopsy of oral mucosal lesions does not cause undue pain in most patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
